1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,085
This is QueueShield. Three payment apps, a central switch, and five banks:

2
00:00:10,135 --> 00:00:15,150
a scaled simulation of a UPI-style network, drawn on an illustrative map.

3
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:20,576
Every dot is a payment. Every number you see comes from our simulator, and the

4
00:00:20,626 --> 00:00:24,090
badges show exactly what is live, mocked, or replayed.

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:28,350
Right now, everything is calm. Let's stress it.

6
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:35,369
First, a festive sale. Demand jumps by sixty percent, the kind of spike that

7
00:00:35,419 --> 00:00:37,530
pages people at most companies.

8
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:45,031
QueueShield stays quiet. Arrivals went up, but latency is stable and queues

9
00:00:45,081 --> 00:00:47,870
drain. That's a surge, not a sick bank.

10
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:54,360
No incident is raised, and no operator is woken up.

11
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:07,180
Now bank B1 slows down, for every payment app at once.

12
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:12,510
Watch the map: the arcs into B1 turn red, and the bank starts to pulse.

13
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:15,473
The localizer names it: bank side, B1.

14
00:01:15,523 --> 00:01:19,600
Not just "errors are up", but which bank, and at what level.

15
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,380
A recommendation arrives: slow down retries and defer status checks, with its

16
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:26,690
supporting signals.

17
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:33,000
An operator approves. The machine recommends; a person decides.

18
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:38,770
Each decision is written to the shared ledger, with a transaction ID and a block

19
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:42,124
number. The ring above the switch is the Drunix ledger.

20
00:01:42,174 --> 00:01:43,710
It flashes on every event.

21
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:50,807
Payments that timed out are not retried blindly.

22
00:01:50,857 --> 00:01:55,425
The bank that knows the outcome attests it once, and every app is pushed the

23
00:01:55,475 --> 00:01:58,950
answer, so the switch is never flooded with status checks.

24
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,548
Here is the safety rule, shown on our test card.

25
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:08,344
When the sick bank is shared by every app, shifting traffic to another app would

26
00:02:08,394 --> 00:02:11,701
only spread the fault. So the console locks that action.

27
00:02:11,751 --> 00:02:12,960
It can never be sent.

28
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:20,220
In 2025, UPI had three outages that NPCI linked to status-check traffic.

29
00:02:20,270 --> 00:02:24,999
This is our replay of that pattern: the same seed, two worlds.

30
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:28,930
On the left, no control. On the right, QueueShield.

31
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:33,769
Both sides see exactly the same traffic, from the same random seed, so any

32
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:36,620
difference you see comes from the policy alone.

33
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:43,965
At about two hundred and fifty seconds, a beneficiary bank starts swallowing

34
00:02:44,015 --> 00:02:44,470
credits.

35
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:52,404
On the left, payments time out, and apps keep asking the switch whether they

36
00:02:52,454 --> 00:02:54,688
went through, hundreds of times a second.

37
00:02:54,738 --> 00:02:58,030
Those questions become the load, and good payments collapse.

38
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:07,991
On the right, the same fault. Checks stay under forty a second, because the bank

39
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:12,510
attests the outcome once on the ledger, and every app is pushed the answer.

40
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:21,457
This is seed three. Across five seeds in our simulator, without control, two

41
00:03:21,507 --> 00:03:25,840
runs locked into a storm like this. With QueueShield, none did.

42
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:33,004
The arena compares every policy on the same seeds, including NPCI's own rule,

43
00:03:33,054 --> 00:03:36,050
which also kept every run out of the storm.

44
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:41,932
And we report where we still lose: today, we use more status checks than the

45
00:03:41,982 --> 00:03:42,660
NPCI rule.

46
00:03:44,410 --> 00:03:47,958
Everything here is a simulation with synthetic data.

47
00:03:48,008 --> 00:03:52,110
Machines recommend, humans decide, and the ledger remembers.
